{"kind":"artifact","text":"The cache witness says which profile transition it actually tests, carried unchanged into the repair head.\n\nExact clean pushed head b1daa56710e5c8df3d2c7d9035682cb9e99f30be on request/signing-standing-repair; tree b6d2c14a4f38890fe08caaf8555cab30273f8a4a, checkout clean, origin matching by commit SHA. Supersedes my candidate c028d5a6 at this path.\n\nWHAT IS HERE, STATED WITHOUT INFLATION. This path is unchanged by the repair commit; the diff at this path between 6bcb4798 and this head is empty. It is republished so the successor covers its predecessor and the lane's artifacts all name one head, not so as to claim new work.\n\nWHAT IT RECORDS. The header block in internal/app/app_test.go named the wrong profile transition: it said @12 to @13 where the test's own constants read oldProfile = workroom-fold@13 and wantProfile = workroom-fold@14. I want the shape of that defect on the record accurately, because I first suspected worse. My initial reading was that the cache test might be vacuous — comparing a profile against itself and proving nothing. It was not. The constants were correct and the test does exercise a real @13-to-@14 transition; only the prose above it lied, in two places. So the correction is to the comment and the second reference, and the behaviour it guards was never broken. Overstating that would have been the easier story and the wrong one.\n\nWHY A COMMENT DESERVED A COMMIT. A witness whose header names a transition it does not test is worse than an unexplained one: the next reader trusts it and stops checking. The value of this test is that a cache keyed on the wrong thing gets caught, and that value is only realisable by someone who can tell from the file which transition is under test.\n\nGates at this exact head: gofmt clean, go build, go vet, go test ./... and internal/docset with -count=1 all pass.","body":{"commit":"b1daa56710e5c8df3d2c7d9035682cb9e99f30be","path":"internal/app"}}